  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 217 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nA strong upper-level trough will impact our local weather through \nthe duration of the extended forecast period. Guidance consensus is \nthat this trough will begin rapidly digging into the eastern half of \nthe country on Wednesday, with an initial shortwave passing through \nthe Middle Mississippi Valley then. Over the last 24-36 hours, a \nmajority of guidance has this wave inducing cyclogenesis somewhere \nover the Central or Southern Plains and tracking the low \nnortheastward through the Midwest. While there is much spread in the \ntrack of this low among guidance, confidence is increasing that it \nor at the very least its cold front will directly impact the CWA. \nThis has led to increasing chances (currently 30-40%) for rainfall \nacross portions of the CWA, with these chances expected to increase \nas guidance spread decreases in the coming days. Both \ninstability and QPF are meager with this system, so severe \nweather and heavy rainfall are not expected. While the forecast \nbeyond Wednesday is dry, additional rounds of light rainfall \ncannot be ruled out through Friday-Saturday as additional \nshortwaves rotate through the trough. At this lead time, \nguidance does a notoriously poor job at accurately resolving \nthese subtle features, hence the dry forecast.\n\nPossibly the bigger story with this trough is a blast of late fall \nair that will move into the region to end the week. With guidance \nconsensus showing an increasingly potent trough, temperatures among \nensembles and deterministic guidance continue to cool. In the wake \nof Wednesday's surface system, below normal temperatures are \nexpected at least Thursday and Friday, with Friday being the \ncooler of the two days. Then, nearly all guidance has a majority\nof the CWA seeing highs in the 40s. Confidence in temperatures \nas we head into the weekend decreases significantly as guidance \ndiverges on the phasing of the trough and how quickly it departs\nthe area. Temperatures will moderate; though how quickly is \nuncertain at this lead time.",
